Here both strings are counted separately. The maps are then compared directly, so the order of the letters does not matter. This is a little more storage than changing one map while scanning `t`, but it makes the final check very clear.

Dry run for `s = "ab"` and `t = "ba"`: the first map becomes `{a=1, b=1}` and the second map becomes `{b=1, a=1}`. Java considers these maps equal, so the result is `true`.

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

class Solution {  
    public boolean anagramStrings(String s, String t) {
        if (s.length() != t.length()) {
            return false;
        }

        Map<Character, Integer> firstCounts = new HashMap<>();
        Map<Character, Integer> secondCounts = new HashMap<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            char firstChar = s.charAt(i);
            char secondChar = t.charAt(i);

            firstCounts.put(firstChar, firstCounts.getOrDefault(firstChar, 0) + 1);
            secondCounts.put(secondChar, secondCounts.getOrDefault(secondChar, 0) + 1);
        }

        return firstCounts.equals(secondCounts);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` on average.
Space complexity: `O(k)`, where `k` is the number of distinct characters stored in the maps.

This version checks each character in `s` against an unused character in `t`. When a match is found, that position in `t` is marked as used. If one character cannot find a match, the strings are not anagrams.

It is not the fastest method, but the matching process is easy to follow and it works without assuming only lowercase letters.

```cpp
class Solution{\t
    public:
        bool anagramStrings(string &s,string &t){
            if (s.size() != t.size()) {
                return false;
            }

            vector<bool> used(t.size(), false);

            for (char sourceChar : s) {
                bool found = false;

                for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(t.size()); i++) {
                    if (!used[i] && t[i] == sourceChar) {
                        used[i] = true;
                        found = true;
                        break;
                    }
                }

                if (!found) {
                    return false;
                }
            }

            return true;
        }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case because each character may scan most of `t`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the used-position array.

Sorting gives a simple way to compare the strings. Anagrams have the same letters, so after sorting, both strings should look exactly the same.

The code sorts copies instead of the input strings, even though the template passes them by reference.

```cpp
class Solution{
    public:
        bool anagramStrings(string &s,string &t){
            if (s.size() != t.size()) {
                return false;
            }

            string sortedS = s;
            string sortedT = t;
            sort(sortedS.begin(), sortedS.end());
            sort(sortedT.begin(), sortedT.end());

            return sortedS == sortedT;
        }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` because both strings are sorted.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the copied strings.

A dictionary works well when the characters are not limited to lowercase letters. The first loop records how often each character appears in `s`. The second loop spends one count for every character in `t`.

If a character is missing or its count has already reached zero, `t` cannot be made from the characters in `s`.

Edge cases such as two empty strings and strings with different lengths are handled before counting.

```python
class Solution:    
    def anagramStrings(self, s, t):
        if len(s) != len(t):
            return False

        counts = {}

        for char in s:
            counts[char] = counts.get(char, 0) + 1

        for char in t:
            if counts.get(char, 0) == 0:
                return False
            counts[char] -= 1

        return True
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` on average for dictionary operations.
Space complexity: `O(k)`, where `k` is the number of different characters.

For lowercase English letters, there are only 26 possible characters. I keep one count for each letter, add the characters from `s`, and remove the characters from `t`. An anagram leaves every count at zero.

For example, with `s = "anagram"` and `t = "nagaram"`, the counts for `a`, `g`, `m`, `n`, and `r` all return to zero.

```java
class Solution {  
    public boolean anagramStrings(String s, String t) {
        if (s.length() != t.length()) {
            return false;
        }

        int[] counts = new int[26];

        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            counts[s.charAt(i) - 'a']++;
            counts[t.charAt(i) - 'a']--;
        }

        for (int count : counts) {
            if (count != 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`, since the array always has 26 entries.

